# nodus/tuvm/tests/target/bridge-health.env — the node half of the porta gate
#
# The bridge is the workload and it certifies itself (design D1): after the mesh
# attaches it asserts the three properties only IT can see — every obs/ cell it
# wrote has been acked by porta, porta's goal/name has converged to its obs/name,
# and its own bounded pending queue has evicted nothing (#streamDropped 0). Then
# it goes on bridging, forever.
#
# What is NOT asserted here, and deliberately: that the tel/log series arrived
# exactly once and contiguously. That is a delivery fact, and a node that dropped
# a row is precisely the node that cannot report it (D4.1) — it belongs to the far
# end, and tests/porta_sync_c6.sh is where it is checked.
#
# class=observe: it reads three numbers the bridge keeps anyway. Nothing here
# writes, reboots, latches or slows anything, which is what would let this same
# scenario be pointed at a live field node once the field deploy exists.
#
# PRECONDITION, asserted rather than assumed. porta must hold a goal/name for
# this node, or result 2 fails saying so. It is one operator write and it
# persists — a cold-booted node pulls from cursor 0 and gets the whole goal tree
# replayed, which is why this needs doing once and not per run:
#
#   ssh gw85224-01 'cd ~/porta-v2 && ./porta put c6-bench goal/name c6-bench --db <the db being served>'
#
# That is not the harness reaching into the gateway (D4 forbids it): a goal/ write
# is the ordinary operator vocabulary, it is what a field node's name is
# configured with, and "does obs match goal" is the convergence view itself.
SCENARIO=bridge-forensics
# rete#222 — the SAME workload as bridge-health, built with the forensics overlay. The node
# cannot see Kconfig and still announces itself as `bridge-health`, so the TAP name is
# overridden rather than the app being taught which build it is (the wedge-note/wedge-detect
# precedent). This row exists to CAPTURE EVIDENCE from a wedge that is expected to happen:
# it will FAIL its observation window on HEAD, and that failure is the point.
TAP_NAME=bridge-health
NODE=PortaBridgeNodeC6.nsl
BOARD_ID=esp32c6
CONF_EXTRA="prj_esp32c6_thread.conf;prj_esp32c6_thread_ftd.conf;prj_esp32c6_forensics.conf"
CLASS=observe
# Boot, then ATTACH — which has been given 90 s since this gate was first
# written — then two check-ins at the 2 s cadence. The node stops waiting at its
# own 180 s deadline and emits the verdict as it stands, so this timeout is the
# outer bound on a workload that has already decided, never the thing that
# decides.
TIMEOUT_S=300

# rete#216 — THE OBSERVATION WINDOW. The TAPs passing is not the verdict: on
# 2026-08-16 this gate returned PASS 3/3 in 22 s against a build that hung four
# ticks later (#213). It scores at tick 2; the node died at tick 6.
#
# 180 s is chosen from the deaths this bench has actually recorded, not picked
# for feel — every one of them lands inside it:
#   #192   tick 2-3   (~6 s)
#   #213   tick 6     (~12 s)
#   #199   2 min 37 s (~157 s) — the arena exhaustion, the longest on record
# and the healthy controls ran to tick 209 and tick 1799 without a stumble, so
# the separation is not subtle. Re-open this number when a death outlives it.
#
# The progress line is the SCENARIO's own — `tick N misses …`, which
# PortaBridgeNodeC6 prints every cadence precisely so a gate can tell running
# from wedged. It has to be a POSITIVE predicate: #213 and #192 both die
# silently, with no fault, no panic and no reboot banner, so no fault grep can
# ever match them. The failure is the absence.
OBSERVE_S=180
OBSERVE_PROGRESS='^.*tick [0-9]+ misses'
